{ The Vienna pawn push attacks your center. Can you find the pawn break that strikes back and frees your pieces? } 3... d5 { Your central break challenges his grip and opens your bishop. [%cal Gd7d5] } (3... Nc6 { The knight develops, but leaves his pawn center unchallenged. } 4. fxe5 4... Nxe5 5. d4 { He keeps more space. Your pawn break attacks the center right away instead. [%cal Rd2d4] }) (3... exf4 { You grab a pawn, but his center can gain time against your knight. } 4. e5 4... Ng8 5. Nf3 { The pawn grab gives him the easier development. Challenge the center with your own break first. [%cal Rg1f3] }) 4. fxe5 { He takes the e pawn instead. } 4... Nxe4 { Your knight gets a central post. That pawn break paid off: his center pawn is gone and your knight owns e4. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... dxe4 { The pawn recapture leaves your other knight under attack. } 5. exf6 5... Nc6 { He leaves f6 for now: a queen recapture would invite a knight attack. } 6. Nxe4 { He gets the better game this way. Recapturing with your knight keeps your pieces active instead. [%cal Rc3e4] }) 0-1


