{ A pawn chain points at your king side. Can you find the pawn break that attacks its base instead of its tip? } 3... c5 { You attack d4, the support of the advanced e pawn. [%cal Gc7c5] } (3... a6 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to a6. } 4. c3 { A different plan: you spend a move on the wing, while your c pawn and knight still want to gang up on d4. [%cal Bc2c3] }) (3... Nc6 { This knight stands in the c pawn doorway. } 4. c3 { Playable, but less flexible. The c pawn wants to challenge d4 before the knight moves. [%cal Bc2c3] }) (3... Bd7 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to d7. } 4. Nf3 { The bishop finds a home, but it is your c pawn and knight that really want to gang up on d4. [%cal Bg1f3] }) (3... Ne7 { Also playable. Your knight steps out to e7 instead. } 4. b4 { A slower plan: b4 grabs space, while your c pawn and knight still want to lean on d4 together. [%cal Bb2b4] }) (3... Nh6 { Also playable. Your knight goes to h6. } 4. c3 { The knight heads for the rim, but the c pawn and knight combination on d4 is still the faster plan. [%cal Bc2c3] }) 4. c3 { He adds a pawn to defend the base. } 4... Nc6 { Your knight joins the pressure your pawn break started. The chain needs constant care now. [%cal Gb8c6] } (4... cxd4 { Also playable. Your pawn captures on d4. } 5. cxd4 { Trading early hands him an easy recapture — keeping the tension and adding a knight to d4 asks more questions. [%cal Bc3d4] }) (4... Bd7 { Also playable. Your bishop settles on d7 instead. } 5. Nf3 { Also fine, but your knight still wants to join the c pawn's pressure on d4 first. [%cal Bg1f3] }) (4... Qb6 { Also good. Your queen adds pressure on d4. } 5. Nf3 { Also good, but your knight is aiming for c6 to add its own pressure on d4. [%cal Bg1f3] }) (4... Ne7 { Also playable. Your knight develops to e7 instead. } 5. Nf3 { This knight route is slower; c6 is the square where it really presses on d4. [%cal Bg1f3] }) (4... Nh6 { Also playable. Your knight heads for the rim instead. } 5. Bb5+ 5... Bd7 6. Bxd7+ 6... Nxd7 { He develops the knight with the recapture, rather than moving his queen or king. } 7. Bxh6 7... gxh6 { Both sides traded a pair of minors, but the c pawn and a knight on c6 still do the real work against d4. [%cal Bg7h6] }) 0-1


