{ His queen went pawn hunting while his king stayed home. Can you open a route to that king? } 8... d5 { Your pawn break opens lines for the developed pieces. His king has no time to relax. [%tail] [%cal Gd7d5] } (8... Na5 { You chase the queen instead of opening the center. } 9. Bxf7+ 9... Kf8 10. Qa2 { The bishop check steals a pawn and drives your king back. Break in the center first. [%cal Rb3a2] }) (8... Ne5 { Also strong. Your knight jumps in with threats. } 9. Nc3 9... Nxe4 10. Nxe4 10... Nxc4 11. Ne2 { He develops instead of taking c4: that capture would allow the d pawn fork. } 11... d5 { Your pawn guards c4 and attacks e4. This also gives you a strong central position. [%cal Gd5c4,Gd5e4] }) (8... d6 { This slow pawn move lets the bishop check and take your rook. } 9. Bxf7+ 9... Kh8 10. Bxe8 10... Qxe8 { He gets a rook and pawn for a bishop. The d5 break avoids that raid. [%cal Rd8e8] }) 9. Bxd5 { He grabs the pawn back with the bishop — a bishop your knight can simply take. [%cal Gc6d5] } 9... Nxd5 { You win a piece for a pawn, and his king still has not found shelter. [%cal Gc6d5] } 0-1


[Event "Lasker plans: Add a pawn to the pileup"]
