{ You want to attack the d pawn. Can you fix the target before lining up your pieces? } 10. cxd5 { You exchange first, so his central pawn becomes a lasting target. [%cal Gc4d5] } (10. Rc1 { Also playable. Your rook goes to c1. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { A useful rook move, but the d pawn was still mobile — fix it with the exchange first. [%cal Bf1c4] }) (10. Nxd5 { That pawn is defended. The knight capture loses material. } 10... exd5 11. cxd5 11... Nxd5 { He takes your knight. Fix the target with the c pawn instead. [%cal Rf6d5] }) (10. Qc2 { Also playable. Your queen goes to c2. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { The queen finds a square, but the d pawn could still run — trade it down first. [%cal Bf1c4] }) (10. Qd2 { Playable, but your queen aims at a pawn that can still move away. } 10... dxc4 { You develop before fixing the target — trading on d5 settles the structure first instead. [%cal Bd5c4] }) (10. Be2 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to e2. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { Development first, but the target kept its options — settle the structure before you aim at it. [%cal Be2c4] }) (10. h3 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to h3. } 10... dxc4 11. Bxc4 { A safe little move, but the d pawn was still free to run — the exchange fixes it in place. [%cal Bf1c4] }) 10... exd5 { He recaptures and keeps material level. } 11. Be2 { Your bishop develops without stepping in front of the d column. [%tail] [%cal Gf1e2] } (11. Rc1 { Also playable. Your rook shifts to c1 instead. } 11... Ne5 { Also useful, but a bishop on e2 keeps the d column free for a rook too. [%cal Bc6e5] }) (11. Qd2 { Also playable. Your queen goes to d2. } 11... a5 { The queen finds a square, but a bishop on e2 stays out of the rook's way on the d column. [%cal Ba6a5] }) (11. Bd3 { Also playable. The bishop develops onto the d column. } 11... Ne5 { Your bishop occupies a future rook road. On e2 it stays out of the way. [%cal Bc6e5] }) (11. h3 { Also playable. Your h pawn steps up instead. } 11... Ne5 { A quiet move, but placing the bishop on e2 keeps the d column open for the rook instead. [%cal Bc6e5] }) 11... Be6 { His bishop develops too, eyeing your queen side. } 12. O-O { Your king tucks away, and the rook on f1 is ready to help too. [%cal Ge1g1] } 12... Qe7 { His queen connects the rooks; the isolated pawn on d5 is still there to be won. } 13. Rc1 { Your rook joins the open column your exchange created, piling one more attacker onto his position. [%cal Ga1c1] } 1-0


