{ Your pieces have more room. Can a pawn move make his bishop on c8 even less comfortable? } 12. f5 { Your pawn takes e6 away from the bishop and frees f4 for your own bishop. [%cal Gf4f5] } (12. Be3 { You develop before restricting his bishop. } 12... Nd5 13. exd5 13... Rxe3 14. dxc6 14... bxc6 { He retains more freedom. The f5 pawn move denies his bishop e6. [%cal Rb7c6] }) 12... b6 { He looks for another diagonal. } 13. Bf4 { Your bishop offers a useful trade. His cramped pieces still need room. [%tail] [%cal Gc1f4] } (13. a3 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to a3. } 13... Bb7 { A useful prep move, but your bishop still wants f4 — denying e6 and eyeing his bishop on d6 together. [%cal Bc8b7] }) (13. Be3 { You retreat from the useful bishop trade. } 13... Nxf5 14. Bxb6 14... Ng3 { He leaves the bishop on b6 and attacks your rook with the knight. } 15. Bxc7 15... Bxc7 { He saves his attacked bishop by taking on c7 instead of taking your rook. } 16. hxg3 { This gives him the easier game. On f4 your bishop challenges his bishop on d6. [%cal Rh2g3] }) (13. Rf2 { Also playable. Your rook goes to f2. } 13... c5 { The rook finds a square, but f4 is still where your bishop wants to be — denying e6 while eyeing his bishop on d6. [%cal Bc6c5] }) (13. g3 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to g3. } 13... Bb7 { A useful luft move, but your bishop still belongs on f4, shutting out e6 and eyeing his bishop on d6. [%cal Bc8b7] }) (13. g4 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to g4. } 13... Bb7 { Space on the wing, but f4 is still the square — your bishop denies e6 and takes aim at his bishop on d6 from there. [%cal Bc8b7] }) 13... Bxf4 { He accepts the trade rather than let the tension sit. } 14. Rxf4 { The cramped bishop is off the board, and you keep the more active minor pieces. [%cal Gf1f4] } 1-0


