{ His bishop wants a diagonal. Can you keep the doubled c pawns from opening one? } 20. c4 { You stop the c5 pawn from advancing. The c6 pawn cannot follow it and free the bishop. [%tail] [%cal Gc2c4] } (20. e4 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to e4. } 20... c4 21. dxc4 { You use the d pawn to make room on its file. The b pawn can recapture too. } 21... dxe4 22. Qxe4 { Also playable — the center opens instead, but your own c4 push already keeps his bishop shut in. [%cal Be2e4] }) (20. Bb2 { Also playable. Your bishop changes its diagonal. } 20... c4 21. dxc4 { Here the d pawn does the recapturing rather than the b pawn — either way, c4 is what keeps the chain fixed. [%cal Bd3c4] }) (20. Nd2 { Also playable. Your knight goes to d2. } 20... Bb7 { The knight repositions, but c4 was the move that actually keeps his bishop shut in. [%cal Ba8b7] }) (20. Kf1 { Also playable. Your king goes to f1. } 20... Bb7 { Safe enough, but c4 is what really keeps his bishop stuck behind its own pawns. [%cal Ba8b7] }) (20. Kf2 { Also playable. Your king goes to f2. } 20... f6 { Also safe, but c4 is the move that keeps his bishop permanently shut out. [%cal Bf7f6] }) (20. Kg2 { Also playable. Your king goes to g2. } 20... c4 21. dxc4 { Tucking the king away is fine, but c4 is what actually keeps his bishop locked in. [%cal Bd3c4] }) (20. h4 { Also playable. You gain ground on the king side. } 20... c4 21. dxc4 { Space on the other wing, and again the d pawn recaptures — c4 is still what keeps the chain fixed. [%cal Bd3c4] }) 20... f6 { He tries to create luft and counterplay on the other wing. } 21. Bb2 { Your bishop steps aside, and the frozen pawns still keep his own bishop shut in on a8. [%cal Gc1b2] } 1-0


