{ Now let us remove the defender. His bishop on g7 guards the dark squares around his king — trade it off with Bh6. [%cal Ye3h6,Gh6g7] } 2. Bh6 { Your bishop offers to remove the guard of the dark squares. [%cal Ge3h6] } (2. Bf4 { Playable too, but this leaves his dark-square guard in place. } 2... dxe5 3. dxe5 { You restore the center, but his dark-square guard is still on the board — that trade can wait for another try. [%cal Bd4e5] }) (2. exd6 { Also playable. Your pawn captures on d6. } 2... cxd6 { A different move order: this removes the bishop only after restoring the center. [%cal Bc7d6] }) 2... dxe5 { He grabs a pawn instead. Take his bishop on g7 before restoring your center. [%cal Yh6g7] } 3. Bxg7 { The dark-square guard is gone. [%cal Gh6g7] } (3. dxe5 { Also playable. You restore the pawn center before trading bishops. } 3... Nb4 { A different move order: the bishop trade on g7 is still there whenever you want it. [%cal Bc6b4] }) 3... Kxg7 { His king recaptures. Take the pawn on e5 with your d pawn. [%cal Yd4e5] } 4. dxe5 { You restore your center. His dark squares no longer have their bishop guard. [%cal Gd4e5] } (4. Nxe5 { The knight recapture leaves your pawn on d4 exposed. } 4... Nxd4 { He takes d4 with the knight, gaining a central post without exposing his queen. } 5. Rad1 5... c5 { He recaptures on d4 instead. Recapturing with your d pawn keeps the center together. [%cal Rc7c5] }) 1-0


[Event "Lasker plans: Give your cramped bishop a job"]
