{ Your pawn chain cramps one bishop. Can you exchange it for a more useful enemy bishop? } 6. Bxe7 { You trade your cramped bishop for his active one. [%cal Gg5e7] } (6. Be3 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to e3. } 6... c5 { A sound alternative, but the mainline already traded off the cramped bishop and is ready to back e5 with f4. [%cal Bc7c5] }) (6. Bf4 { You keep the bishop behind your own pawn chain. } 6... c5 7. dxc5 7... Nc6 { He develops his other knight before taking c5. Your cramped bishop still needs a job. [%cal Rb8c6] }) (6. h4 { Also playable. Your pawn goes to h4. } 6... h6 { He attacks your bishop with a pawn instead of exchanging immediately on g5. } 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { Sound too, and after the trade you are back to the very same plan: support e5 with f4. [%cal Bd8e7] }) 6... Qxe7 { His queen steps in to recapture. } 7. f4 { Your f pawn reinforces e5 and leaves the knight free to choose its route. [%cal Gf2f4] } (7. Nb5 { Also sound. You clear c3 so the pawn can support d4. } 7... Nb6 { A different plan: hold the base of the chain with the c pawn instead. [%cal Bd7b6] }) (7. Qd2 { Also sound. The queen prepares a knight retreat and c3. } 7... O-O { This prepares to support d4, while the mainline develops the king side and supports e5 right away. [%cal Be8g8] }) (7. Bd3 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to d3. } 7... Qb4 { A sound try, but the mainline already traded the cramped bishop and is backing e5 with f4 right now. [%cal Be7b4] }) (7. Nf3 { Also playable. You develop the knight immediately. } 7... O-O { Your knight now blocks f4, where the mainline supports e5 with the pawn before developing the knight. [%cal Be8g8] }) (7. h4 { Also playable. Your pawn advances on the wing. } 7... O-O { Also sound, though the mainline is already trading the cramped bishop and backing e5 with f4. [%cal Be8g8] }) 1-0


