{ His f pawn has moved. Can you remove the defender before organizing pressure on e6? } 13. Bxe7 { You remove his bishop, then prepare to bring the rooks to the e column. [%cal Gg5e7] } (13. Be3 { Also playable. Your bishop settles on e3 instead. } 13... Qd5 { A useful try, but the mainline already removes the defender and heads for the e column with a castle. [%cal Bd8d5] }) (13. Bf4 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to f4. } 13... Nf6 { Also useful, though the mainline already trades off the defender before castling onto the e column. [%cal Be8f6] }) 13... Qxe7 { His queen has to take over the bishop job. } 14. O-O { Your king is safe, and your rook on f1 already eyes the e column. [%tail] [%cal Ge1g1] } (14. Bc2 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to c2. } 14... Qg5 { A useful try, but castling straight onto the e column gets there faster. [%cal Be7g5] }) (14. O-O-O { Also good. You put your king on the other wing. } 14... c5 { Both castling choices are sound here. The king side choice frees the e rook quickly. [%cal Bc6c5] }) (14. f4 { Also playable. Your pawn reinforces e5. } 14... a5 { You support the center first, while castling connects the rooks sooner. [%cal Ba7a5] }) (14. g4 { Also playable. Your g pawn advances instead. } 14... c5 15. dxc5 15... Qxc5 { Also useful, but the mainline is already castling and heading for the open e column. [%cal Be7c5] }) (14. Qe3 { Also playable. Your queen goes to e3. } 14... Nc7 { A useful try, but castling and swinging the rook to e1 is the more direct route. [%cal Be8c7] }) 14... c5 { He challenges the center rather than defend passively. } 15. Rfe1 { Your rook lands on the e column, adding real pressure next to your knight. [%cal Gf1e1] } 1-0


