{ You can exchange minor pieces. Which trade avoids helping a pawn chase your other knight? } 6. Bxe7 { You exchange bishops without drawing his d pawn onto e4. [%cal Gg5e7] } (6. Nxe4 { Also sound, despite the concern about the pawn landing on e4. } 6... dxe4 { He restores material with the d pawn rather than starting a bishop exchange. } 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { The pawn controls f3, but your position stays playable — trading bishops right away avoids that structure. [%cal Bd8e7] }) (6. Qa4+ { Also playable. Your queen goes to a4. } 6... Bd7 7. Bxe7 7... Qxe7 { Also sound, though trading bishops right away is what keeps f3 clear for your knight on g1. [%cal Bd8e7] }) (6. Bf4 { Also playable. Your bishop settles behind the pawns on f4 instead. } 6... c5 7. cxd5 7... Nxc3 { He trades knights before recovering the d5 pawn. } 8. bxc3 8... Qa5 { He pins c3 before recovering d5 — trading bishops immediately sidesteps that extra pressure. [%cal Bd8a5] }) (6. h4 { Also playable. You gain space with the h pawn. } 6... Bxg5 7. hxg5 7... Nxc3 { He exchanges knights before collecting the pawn on g5. } 8. bxc3 8... Qxg5 { Also sound, but trading bishops immediately is the cleaner way to keep f3 clear for the knight. [%cal Bd8g5] }) 6... Qxe7 { His queen recaptures, ready to meet whatever comes next. } 7. Qc2 { Your queen supports the center, and f3 is still open for your other knight. [%tail] [%cal Gd1c2] } (7. Rc1 { Also playable. Your rook heads to c1 instead. } 7... O-O { You organize your pieces differently, while the queen on c2 already helps control the center. [%cal Be8g8] }) (7. Nxe4 { Also playable. You accept the pawn on e4. } 7... dxe4 { It controls f3, but this is not a tactical mistake — the queen move to c2 keeps f3 free instead. [%cal Bd5e4] }) (7. cxd5 { Also good. You change the central pawn structure first. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 8... exd5 { Another useful plan, though the queen move to c2 keeps more pawn tension on the board for now. [%cal Be6d5] }) (7. Qb3 { Also playable. Your queen goes to b3. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 { You develop the bishop with the recapture instead of moving your queen again. } 8... Nxc3 9. Qxc3 { You take with the queen and keep your pawns together, though c2 was the tidier square for her. [%cal Bb3c3] }) (7. Bd3 { Also playable. Your bishop develops to d3 instead. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 { You organize your pieces differently here too, while c2 is still the square that helps control the center. [%cal Bd3c4] }) (7. Nge2 { Also playable. Your knight goes to e2. } 7... Nxc3 8. Nxc3 { This develops the last knight, while the queen on c2 is what really helps control the center. [%cal Be2c3] }) (7. Nf3 { Also playable. Your knight goes to f3. } 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 { This develops the knight a move early, while the queen on c2 is what really helps control the center. [%cal Bb2c3] }) 7... Nxc3 { He trades knights rather than let the tension sit. } 8. Qxc3 { You recapture and keep the center solid. [%cal Gc2c3] } 8... O-O { He tucks his king away, the position steady. } 9. Nf3 { Your knight on g1 finally reaches f3 — the square you avoided blocking three moves ago. [%cal Gg1f3] } 1-0


