{ Now let us open lines against his king: a hook is an enemy pawn you attack to help force a file open. Push to h5 and use g6 as your hook. [%cal Yh4h5,Gh5g6,Gh1h7] } 2. h5 { Your pawn attacks the g6 hook, and his king side is about to come under real pressure. [%tail] [%cal Gh4h5] } (2. c3 { Also playable. Your c pawn prepares first instead. } 2... h6 { A quieter plan — the h5 push was already asking real questions of his king side pawns. [%cal Bh7h6] }) (2. O-O { Playable, but castling moves your rook away from the h column. } 2... e5 { Castling is safe, but it was the h5 push that put his king side under immediate pressure. [%cal Be7e5] }) (2. e4 { Also playable. Your e pawn claims more space instead. } 2... c5 { Also solid, though the h5 push was already the move making him find answers on the king side. [%cal Bc7c5] }) (2. Bg5 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to g5. } 2... c6 { Also natural, but the h5 push was already the one creating real problems for him. [%cal Bc7c6] }) 2... Nxh5 { He grabs the pawn — the critical test, and the sharpest way to meet your hook. } 3. Rxh5 { You take back the knight, offering the exchange right back to rip his king side open. [%cal Gh1h5] } 3... gxh5 { He recaptures with the pawn, keeping the extra material for now. } 4. Bxh7+ { Your bishop crashes in, and his king shelter is gone for good. [%cal Gd3h7] } 4... Kf8 { His king steps aside rather than grab a second piece. } 5. Ne4 { Your knight joins the attack — real, lasting pressure for the material you gave. [%cal Gd2e4] } 1-0


