{ His king fled to the queen side. Can you open lines toward its new shelter? } 11. a4 { Your pawn break follows the king to its new wing. [%cal Ga2a4] } (11. Rad1 { Also playable. Your rook goes to d1. } 11... c4 { Another useful developing move, but the a pawn push is what actually opens a column toward his king on d8. [%cal Bc5c4] }) (11. a3 { Also playable. You take a smaller step with the a pawn. } 11... Rf7 { A slower version of the same idea — the full a4 push opens the column toward his king faster. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (11. b3 { Also playable. Your b pawn steps forward instead. } 11... Rf7 { A different wing pawn move, but it is the a pawn that opens the column toward his king on d8. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (11. b4 { Also playable. You gain queen side space instead. } 11... c4 { Another useful try, but the a pawn push is the one that actually opens a column toward his king on d8. [%cal Bc5c4] }) (11. Bd1 { Also playable. Your bishop goes to d1. } 11... c4 { The bishop finds a new square, but the a pawn push is what opens the column toward his king on d8. [%cal Bc5c4] }) (11. Ne1 { Also playable. Your knight goes to e1. } 11... Bb7 { The knight repositions, but the a pawn push is what actually opens the column toward his king on d8. [%cal Bc8b7] }) 11... Bb7 { He places the bishop on the long diagonal. } 12. axb5 { You open the a column. [%cal Ga4b5] } (12. Ra2 { Also playable. Your rook goes to a2. } 12... Nd6 { Also playable, though trading pawns on b5 opens the a column right away instead. [%cal Be8d6] }) (12. b3 { Also playable. You support c4 with the b pawn instead. } 12... Nd6 { Also playable, but trading on b5 is what opens the a column immediately. [%cal Be8d6] }) (12. b4 { Also playable. Your b pawn pushes on instead. } 12... Rc8 { Also playable, though the pawn trade on b5 opens the a column right away. [%cal Ba8c8] }) (12. Ne1 { Also playable. Your knight tucks back to e1 instead. } 12... Nd6 { Also playable, but trading pawns on b5 is the move that opens the